<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Tadanao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1595–1650</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Tadanao was the second lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> domain in Echizen, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuki-hideyasu">Yuki Hideyasu</a>. He fought at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615 but became resentful of his treatment by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. In 1623 he was removed for misconduct and exiled to Bungo, and the Fukui domain was reduced.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-tadanao.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Tadanao" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 伝忠直筆 / Allegedly a self-portrait · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsudaira_Tadanao_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
